CORE
main.cpp
--cpp17 --unwind 6 --no-unwinding-assertions
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ tuple get<0> reads the int element: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ tuple get<1> reads the double element: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG must FAIL: FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
std::get on a directly-constructed two-element std::tuple<int,double>.  The
second element's _Head_base is a non-first base whose byte offset depends on
the differently-sized first element, so std::get<1> requires the data-only
base-subobject offset to read the correct subobject.  Non-vacuous: the int
operand is nondet and the last assertion is a deliberately wrong claim.
